# Line endings are a repository property, not a per-developer one.
#
# Without this file the line ending a blob gets is decided by whoever commits
# it: `core.autocrlf` and `core.eol` are user-level settings, and a Windows
# contributor whose editor writes CRLF commits CRLF. Measured over raw
# `git cat-file` blobs on this base: 373 tracked files, 17 binary, 356 text,
# **6 pure CRLF**, 0 mixed, 0 lone CR. The six are the 2025
# `.spec-workflow/templates/*.md`.
#
# The cost is silent diff inflation. Every untouched line in such a file is
# rewritten with a different terminator, so a small edit is recorded as a
# rewrite and the real change disappears into it.
#
# The `mixed-line-ending` pre-commit hook does NOT catch this. Its default
# `--fix=auto` only acts on files that mix terminators, and normalises those
# to their own majority; a file that is consistently CRLF is not mixed, so the
# hook returns 0 without printing anything. `.pre-commit-config.yaml` now
# pins `--fix=lf` so the hook enforces the same rule this file does.
#
# `text=auto` leaves git's binary detection in charge, so the .png and .whl
# fixtures are untouched; they are listed explicitly below anyway, because an
# explicit marker survives a future change to the detection heuristic.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary fixtures: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.whl binary

# Map data is text and is normalised with everything else. Listed explicitly
# because these are large and a reviewer reaching for this file will want to
# see that the decision was made deliberately rather than by omission.
*.osm text eol=lf
*.xodr text eol=lf
